WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.718 --> 00:00:16.080
Phileas Fogg's five whist opponents all looked at each other, nodded, and said unanimously, "We accept!"

2
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.961
Mr. Fogg's wager to travel around the world in eighty days was on!

3
00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:23.782
"Good," said Phileas Fogg.

4
00:00:23.802 --> 00:00:27.483
"The train leaves for Dover at a quarter before nine.

5
00:00:28.743 --> 00:00:29.303
I will take it."

6
00:00:29.323 --> 00:00:31.104
"This very evening?"

7
00:00:31.484 --> 00:00:32.324
one of the men said.

8
00:00:32.344 --> 00:00:34.505
"Yes, this very evening!"

9
00:00:34.885 --> 00:00:35.365
said Mr.

10
00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:41.187
Fogg. He then took out his pocket almanac and added, "Today is Wednesday, October 2.

11
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:47.609
I shall be due in London, in this very room of the Reform Club, on Saturday December 21,

12
00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:50.230
at a quarter before 9:00 p.m.

13
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:58.052
If I am late, twenty-thousand pounds, now deposited in my name at Baring's Bank, will belong to you."

14
00:00:59.612 --> 00:01:00.152
Then Mr.

15
00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:03.514
Fogg pulled out his checkbook and signed a check with his fountain pen.

16
00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:08.377
"In fact, gentlemen," he said, "here is a check for the amount."

17
00:01:09.538 --> 00:01:13.440
The men drafted a contract outlining the wager and everyone signed it.

18
00:01:15.521 --> 00:01:19.643
During this whole exchange, Phileas Fogg maintained his usual cool composure.

19
00:01:21.144 --> 00:01:24.206
He had not bet to win, but merely to live up to his word.

20
00:01:25.466 --> 00:01:28.468
He had staked twenty-thousand pounds, half his fortune.

21
00:01:29.696 --> 00:01:33.339
He thought he might have to spend the other half to carry out this difficult project.

22
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.043
The clock struck seven, and the group agreed to suspend the game so Mr.

23
00:01:39.063 --> 00:01:40.704
Fogg could prepare for his departure.

24
00:01:40.724 --> 00:01:43.586
"It is unnecessary, gentlemen.

25
00:01:45.127 --> 00:01:46.948
I am quite ready now," was Mr.

26
00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:47.789
Fogg's response.

27
00:01:47.809 --> 00:01:51.852
"It is your turn, I believe," he said to the man on his left.

28
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:59.022
So, after winning twenty guineas at whist and taking leave of his friends, Phileas Fogg, at twenty-five minutes

29
00:01:59.062 --> 00:02:02.563
past seven, left the Reform Club and made his way home.

30
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:04.703
When Mr.

31
00:02:04.743 --> 00:02:07.484
Fogg arrived home, Passepartout was surprised.

32
00:02:08.424 --> 00:02:11.025
His master was not due until exactly midnight.

33
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:15.206
Mr. Fogg immediately went to his room and called for his servant.

34
00:02:16.527 --> 00:02:18.127
But Passepartout did not reply.

35
00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:20.348
Mr. Fogg could not be calling for him.

36
00:02:20.728 --> 00:02:21.628
It was not the right hour!

37
00:02:22.432 --> 00:02:24.413
"Passepartout," repeated Mr.

38
00:02:24.453 --> 00:02:25.953
Fogg, without raising his voice.

39
00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:28.254
Passepartout arrived.

40
00:02:28.274 --> 00:02:31.114
"I've called you twice," said his master.

41
00:02:31.134 --> 00:02:35.236
"But it is not midnight," said Passepartout, showing Mr.

42
00:02:35.256 --> 00:02:36.036
Fogg his watch.

43
00:02:36.056 --> 00:02:37.516
"I know that.

44
00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:42.958
I don't blame you, but we start for Dover and Calais in ten minutes," said Mr.

45
00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:45.398
Fogg. "You are going to leave home?"

46
00:02:45.839 --> 00:02:48.299
asked Passepartout with a puzzled look on his face.

47
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.600
"Yes," said his master.

48
00:02:51.724 --> 00:02:53.505
"We are going around the world."

49
00:02:55.226 --> 00:02:58.907
Passepartout opened his eyes wide, and looked like he might collapse.

50
00:02:58.927 --> 00:03:01.948
"Around the world?"

51
00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:02.829
he murmured.

52
00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:06.210
"In eighty days," replied Mr.

53
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.851
Fogg. "So we haven't a moment to lose."

54
00:03:08.871 --> 00:03:11.872
"But I must pack our trunks!"

55
00:03:12.273 --> 00:03:13.313
Passepartout said.

56
00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:19.215
"We will have no trunks, only a carpetbag with two shirts and three pairs of socks for me,

57
00:03:19.596 --> 00:03:20.236
and the same for you.

58
00:03:21.208 --> 00:03:23.669
We will buy our clothes on the way," said Mr.

59
00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:26.771
Fogg. Passepartout tried to reply but couldn't.

60
00:03:27.592 --> 00:03:28.232
He left Mr.

61
00:03:28.272 --> 00:03:32.534
Fogg's room, climbed the stairs to his own room, and fell into the closest chair.

62
00:03:33.995 --> 00:03:35.156
Was his master a fool?

63
00:03:36.296 --> 00:03:37.697
No. Was this a joke?

64
00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:39.198
It didn't seem so.

65
00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:42.900
They were going to Dover and then to Calais, in France.

66
00:03:43.921 --> 00:03:45.522
Passepartout thought a moment later.

67
00:03:46.422 --> 00:03:49.644
After all, Passepartout had been away from France for five years.

68
00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.561
He would not be sorry to see his native soil again.

69
00:03:53.182 --> 00:03:59.905
Remembering there was no time to waste, Passepartout packed the carpetbag and was ready by eight o'clock.

70
00:04:01.186 --> 00:04:02.547
Mr. Fogg was ready as well.

71
00:04:03.407 --> 00:04:06.429
He was carrying the railway and steamer timetable under his arm.

72
00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.612
He then took the carpetbag, opened it, and slipped a huge roll of bank notes inside it.

73
00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:15.073
"You have forgotten nothing?"

74
00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:16.594
he asked Passepartout.

75
00:04:16.614 --> 00:04:20.480
"No, nothing, sir," replied Passepartout.

76
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.381
"Good! Take this carpetbag," Mr.

77
00:04:22.421 --> 00:04:24.402
Fogg said, handing it to Passepartout.

78
00:04:24.422 --> 00:04:28.385
"Take good care of it because there is twenty-thousand pounds in it."

79
00:04:29.525 --> 00:04:34.268
Passepartout nearly dropped the bag, as if the twenty- thousand pounds were as heavy as gold.

80
00:04:35.589 --> 00:04:40.892
Mr. Fogg and Passepartout then left the house, locked the door securely, and took a cab to Charing

81
00:04:40.952 --> 00:04:42.153
Cross railway station.

82
00:04:44.694 --> 00:04:50.246
The cab stopped in front of the station at 8:25, exactly twenty- five-minutes before the train was to

83
00:04:50.266 --> 00:04:55.670
depart. Oh my!

84
00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.332
I had thought I would be settling down for a nice, quiet life.

85
00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:05.117
But suddenly I found out I was about to travel around the world—and in eighty days!

86
00:05:05.657 --> 00:05:08.079
Mr. Fogg told me that our trip will start in Dover.

87
00:05:08.719 --> 00:05:14.183
Located at the southeastern tip of England, Dover is famous for its tall, white cliffs, which are made

88
00:05:14.203 --> 00:05:15.164
of pure white chalk.

89
00:05:16.192 --> 00:05:21.534
In fact, these white cliffs gave England its most ancient name: Albion, which means "white."

90
00:05:22.654 --> 00:05:25.375
Dover is one of the busiest places to cross the English Channel.

91
00:05:26.155 --> 00:05:29.436
Traffic through this port town has boomed since the railway connected it to London.

92
00:05:30.176 --> 00:05:35.937
People traveling on the London-Dover train can take the 21-mile ferry ride across the English Channel to Calais,

93
00:05:35.997 --> 00:05:39.438
France. There they can hop on the Calais-Paris train.

94
00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:42.659
So far our journey around the world sounds easy, right?

95
00:05:43.179 --> 00:05:44.900
Well, we'll see what happens next!
